ABSTRACT
\f1\b0 \

\f0\b Title:
\f1\b0  Synchronized Surface Operation Dynamics (SSOD) Axle Management System for Heavy Vehicles\

\f0\b Text:
\f1\b0 \
A system and method for dynamic axle management in heavy vehicles, designed to eliminate rolling resistance and mechanical inertia during axle transitions. The system comprises a primary drive axle (Axle A) and a secondary dynamic axle (Axle B) equipped with electric hub motors, a high-voltage supercapacitor for energy storage, and a hydraulic actuation system. The system utilizes a master control unit (ECU) configured to execute a predictive synchronization protocol (
\f0\b e-Sync
\f1\b0 ) that accelerates the standby axle to match road speed prior to surface contact, and a regenerative phase (
\f0\b KERS
\f1\b0 ) to recover kinetic energy from the rising axle.\
The system is characterized by a synchronization index (
\f0\b Phi
\f1\b0 ) calculation that accounts for the 
\f0\b dynamic tyre radius (R_dynamic)
\f1\b0  and vehicle speed, and an anticipatory hydraulic control logic that compensates for mechanical latency (
\f0\b Delta-t
\f1\b0 ). A hardware-level fail-safe is provided by a mechanical interlock mechanism comprising a 
\f0\b 42CrMo4
\f1\b0  steel rocker arm. The integration of 
\f0\b ASIL-D
\f1\b0  compliant watchdog monitoring and 
\f0\b PWM-controlled
\f1\b0  hydraulics ensures high operational safety, reduced tyre wear, and optimized energy recovery during transition phases.\
\
\
